/**
 * Easter-egg overlays: Mission Control (the solar-system launcher) and the
 * nine game modals. Hidden until the Polaris star in the hero is clicked.
 *
 * `#egg` through `#egg7` are hand-drawn canvas games living in
 * `eggs/runtime.ts`.
 *
 * `#egg8` and `#egg9` are the two that are not: both are Phaser games under
 * `src/games/`, mounted into `#egg8-mount` and `#egg9-mount` by a dynamic
 * import, so neither Phaser nor the artwork is fetched until the planet is
 * activated.
 *
 *   - `#egg8` — Mercury / SOLAR CIRCUIT, `src/games/solar-road/`. A port of
 *     Phaser3-Road (MIT); see public/eggs/solar-road/ATTRIBUTION.md. It
 *     replaced a hand-drawn canvas racer of the same name in Aug 2026.
 *   - `#egg9` — Uranus / POLARIS-MAN, `src/games/polaris-man/`. It took slot 9
 *     rather than 8 because SOLAR CIRCUIT claimed 8 on its branch first.
 *
 * The two differ in one respect worth knowing: Polaris-Man owns its own audio
 * graph and its own keyboard, so its ✕/mute route through the game handle.
 * SOLAR CIRCUIT has neither, so it keeps the site's EggAudio and the ordinary
 * `eggToggleMute()` button, exactly like the canvas eggs.
 *
 * The markup is carried over verbatim from the POC — the galaxy map is a
 * hand-tuned SVG whose geometry the runtime animates by node reference, so it
 * is emitted as raw HTML rather than re-authored as JSX-ish Astro markup.
 *
 * Mission Control now launches from the three.js solar system in
 * `scripts/eggs/solar3d.ts`, which mounts into `#ss3d`. The SVG map it
 * replaced is still here, in `#ss-legacy`, hidden rather than deleted: it is
 * also the fallback the runtime reveals if WebGL is unavailable, so the seven
 * games stay reachable on a machine that cannot render the 3D scene. The
 * canvases and animation code behind it are untouched in `eggs/runtime.ts`.
 *
 * Mission Control is a framed panel at 90% of the viewport, not a full-bleed
 * render: a titled box with the retro Mersive logo film as its opening, then the
 * system inside the same frame. The chrome is part of the panel rather than
 * floating over the scene — "You Found Polaris" and the mute toggle in the head,
 * "Explore the planets" and the key hints in the foot — so the render has a
 * defined edge and the 5% of backdrop around it reads as the room it sits in.
 *
 * The film is video-only: the source's audio track measured -91 dB, i.e. digital
 * silence, so it was stripped. Sound over the logo is a synth sting from
 * EggAudio, and the score starts with the system.
 */
